      "content": "A rogue protein or trace impurity acting as a bridging agent is a very plausible scenario; those molecules are notorious for being the tiny, mischievous saboteurs in food chemistry. They can adsorb onto multiple droplets simultaneously, effectively creating the 'sticky net' that leads to flocculation. To identify this, you'd need analytical tools that can detect low concentrations of foreign matter, perhaps chromatography or advanced spectroscopy to check for unexpected macromolecules.\n\nIf you suspect a contaminant, you could try purifying your ingredients or running control batches with highly purified versions of your oils and acids. Sometimes, the very raw materials themselves carry the seeds of instability, much like a single, poorly behaved spore can infect an entire colony of mold.",
